//ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/context/flr/article/2526/viewcontent/flr50.52.Project.pdf","t":"The Lessons of Creation-Science: Public School Curriculum and the Religion Clauses","tp":["religion","speech"],"u":"https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/flr/vol50/iss6/3","v":50,"y":1982},{"a":["Richard F. Hahn"],"ab":"","c":18,"fp":957,"i":5,"id":"colr-18445","j":"colr","t":"Constitutional Limits on the Power to Exclude Aliens","tp":["citizenship"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1122212","v":82,"y":1982},{"a":["Jacob A. Manheimer"],"ab":"","i":6,"id":"flr-6659","j":"flr","kw":["federal rights","fugitives","interstate rendition","section 1983","damages","constitution","Ku Klux Klan Act","recognition of action"],"pdf":"https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/context/flr/article/2531/viewcontent/flr50.57.Notes.pdf","t":"Interstate Rendition Violations and Section 1983: Locating the Federal Rights of Fugitives","tp":["remedies","federalism","method"],"u":"https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/flr/vol50/iss6/8","v":50,"y":1982},{"a":["R. C. S."],"ab":"","fp":589,"i":3,"id":"valr-20150","j":"valr","t":"Fourth Amendment Dispute Types and the Scope of Stone v. Powell","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072855","v":68,"y":1982,"c":0},{"a":["David Martin"],"ab":"","c":4,"fp":253,"i":2,"id":"valr-19663","j":"valr","t":"The Legislative Veto and the Responsible Exercise of Congressional Power","tp":["congress"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072881","v":68,"y":1982},{"a":["L. Harold Levinson"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"wmlr-8355","j":"wmlr","kw":["Administrative Procedure","Executive Power","Legislative Power","Veto","Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.wm.edu/context/wmlr/article/2235/viewcontent/wmlr24_no1_p79_levinson.pdf","t":"Legislative and Executive Veto of Rules of Administrative Agencies: Models and Alternatives","tp":["admin","congress","executive"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.wm.edu/wmlr/vol24/iss1/3","v":24,"y":1982},{"a":["William A. Fletcher"],"ab":"","c":54,"fp":635,"i":4,"id":"ylj-16680","j":"ylj","t":"The Discretionary Constitution: Institutional Remedies and Judicial Legitimacy","tp":["democratic"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/796035","v":91,"y":1982,"oa":"https://openyls.law.yale.edu/bitstream/20.500.13051/16130/2/40_91YaleLJ635_1981_1982_.pdf"},{"a":["Philip Halpern"],"ab":"In Stone","c":1,"fp":1,"i":1,"id":"colr-18662","j":"colr","t":"Federal Habeas Corpus and the Mapp Exclusionary Rule after Stone v. Powell","tp":["security","crimpro"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1122237","v":82,"y":1982,"oa":"https://digitalcommons.law.buffalo.edu/context/journal_articles/article/1102/viewcontent/Halpern_Federal_20Habeas_20Corpus.pdf","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Benno Schmidt"],"ab":"","c":40,"fp":646,"i":4,"id":"colr-18373","j":"colr","t":"Principle and Prejudice: The Supreme Court and Race in the Progressive Era. Part 2: The \"Peonage Cases\"","tp":["equality","court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1122195","v":82,"y":1982},{"a":["Enid Greene"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"byulr-10013","j":"byulr","kw":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law","Law"],"pdf":"https://digitalcommons.law.byu.edu/context/lawreview/article/1318/viewcontent/1982BYUL.Rev.192_204.pdf","t":"Double Jeopardy and Resentencing in Bifurcated Criminal Proceedings: Bullington v. Missouri","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://digitalcommons.law.byu.edu/lawreview/vol1982/iss1/6","v":1982,"y":1982},{"a":["Benno Schmidt"],"ab":"","c":11,"fp":444,"i":3,"id":"colr-18403","j":"colr","t":"Principle and Prejudice: The Supreme Court and Race in the Progressive Era. Part 1: The Heyday of Jim Crow","tp":["equality","court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1122071","v":82,"y":1982},{"a":["Harold H. Bruff"],"ab":"IT is tempting to regard the law that governs -the Presidency as flowing mostly from the handful of great constitutional cases that have infused article II with meaning.'Of course, no one would deny that these cases mark the outer limits of legitimate presidential action, and no one should underestimate their real capacity to constrain executive decisionmaking.Nevertheless, most day-to-day questions of presidential power depend in large part on questions of statutory authority.In litigation challenging presidential actions, the Government often places exclusive reliance on statutory powers.'Moreover, when constitutional issues are intermixed with statutory ones, courts ordinarily examine the statutory issues first, in hopes of avoiding a decision based on the Constitution.'Although litigation involving the President's implementation of his…","c":5,"fp":1,"i":1,"id":"valr-19660","j":"valr","t":"Judicial Review and the President's Statutory Powers","tp":["executive","court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072704","v":68,"y":1982,"oa":"https://scholar.law.colorado.edu/context/faculty-articles/article/2079/viewcontent/1982_Bruff_Judicial_Review_and_the_President_s_Statutory_Powers.pdf","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Lillian R. BeVier"],"ab":"","c":1,"fp":177,"i":2,"id":"valr-19929","j":"valr","t":"Justice Powell and the First Amendment's \"Societal Function\": A Preliminary Analysis","tp":["court","speech"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072879","v":68,"y":1982},{"a":["Paul B. Stephan"],"ab":"","c":8,"fp":203,"i":2,"id":"valr-19709","j":"valr","t":"The First Amendment and Content Discrimination","tp":["speech","equality"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072880","v":68,"y":1982},{"a":["Richard L. Musick"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"byulr-9953","j":"byulr","kw":["Commercial Law","Constitutional Law","Law","Taxation-State and Local"],"pdf":"https://digitalcommons.law.byu.edu/context/lawreview/article/1337/viewcontent/1982BYUL.Rev.765_777.pdf","t":"A Deferential Interpretation of the Complete Auto Transit Test for Determining Whether a State Tax Unconstitutionally Burdens Interstate Commerce: Commonwealth Edison Co. v. Montana","tp":["federalism","econreg"],"u":"https://digitalcommons.law.byu.edu/lawreview/vol1982/iss3/8","v":1982,"y":1982},{"a":["Myron D. Rumeld"],"ab":"","c":1,"fp":1183,"i":6,"id":"colr-18649","j":"colr","t":"Preclusion of Section 1983 Causes of Action by Comprehensive Statutory Remedial Schemes","tp":["remedies"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1122163","v":82,"y":1982},{"a":["Robert B. Keiter"],"ab":"","c":2,"id":"mlr_minn-6173","j":"mlr_minn","kw":["Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.umn.edu/context/mlr/article/3226/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Privacy, Children, and Their Parents: Reflections On and Beyond the Supreme Court's Approach","tp":["court","dueprocess"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.umn.edu/mlr/2227","y":1982},{"a":["William B. Spong","W. Taylor Reveley"],"ab":"","fp":1437,"i":7,"id":"valr-19697","j":"valr","rv":1,"t":"War Powers of the President and Congress: Who Holds the Arrows and Olive Branch?","tp":["security","executive","congress"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1072734","v":68,"y":1982,"cx":1},{"a":["III, William J. Whelan"],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"flr-7000","j":"flr","kw":["public records","press","public","judicial branch","public access","monitor","first amendment","trial"],"pdf":"https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/context/flr/article/4555/viewcontent/flr50.31.Notes.pdf","t":"Copying and Broadcasting Video and Audio Tape Evidence: A Threat to the Fair Trial Right","tp":["speech"],"u":"https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/flr/vol50/iss4/3","v":50,"y":1982},{"a":["Julian N. Eule"],"ab":"Judging from the numbers alone, the dormant commerce clause' seems to be experiencing a renaissance.During the years 1953 through 1975, the United States Supreme Court issued only eight opinions that cont Professor of Law, Temple University.I…","c":32,"fp":425,"i":3,"id":"ylj-16712","j":"ylj","t":"Laying the Dormant Commerce Clause to Rest","tp":["federalism","congress"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/795926","v":91,"y":1982,"oa":"https://openyls.law.yale.edu/bitstream/20.500.13051/16123/2/32_91YaleLJ425_1981_1982_.pdf"},{"a":["Jr., Robert C. Brighton"],"ab":"This Note reviews the history of American federalism with particular emphasis on a comparison of the reality of the birth of American federalism with the myth that shrouds that event. The interplay of the several levels of American government in the federal system has been called \"cooperative federalism.\" The Note describes cooperative federalism as a process and criticizes the use of federalism as a rationale for the implementation of policy decisions.' The Note next considers the Supreme Court's current analysis of federalism issues that have arisen when Congress has exercised its powers under the commerce clause and identifies the contradictions and confusions that result when myth and reality are intermingled in the Court's analysis.","c":0,"i":1,"id":"vlr-5613","j":"vlr","kw":["American Federalism","cooperative federalism","policy decisions","commerce clause","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2842/viewcontent/12_35VandLRev161_1982_.pdf","t":"Separating Myth from Reality in Federalism Decisions: A Perspective of American Federalism--Past and Present","tp":["federalism","congress"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss1/3","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["Luther L. McDougal L. McDougal III"],"ab":"This Article examines whether the courts should shift their focus to a policy or interest analysis in judicial jurisdiction cases. The Article initially identifies some of the theoretical inadequacies of, and practical difficulties with, the Supreme Court's purposefully availing/minimum contacts approach to jurisdictional problems. The Article then outlines an alternative approach that employs a comprehensive form of interest analysis. After setting forth this proposed framework, the Article proceeds to examine Supreme Court decisions since International Shoe from the standpoint of the outcomes that an interest analysis approach might produce. Finally, the Article appraises these varying results and recommends that the courts employ an approach that more realistically relates to people's everyday activities and more accurately reflects contemporary goals and policies.","i":1,"id":"vlr-5612","j":"vlr","kw":["judicial jurisdiction","policy analysis","interest analysis","Jurisdiction","Law","Supreme Court of the United States"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2839/viewcontent/09_35VandLRev1_1982_.pdf","t":"Judicial Jurisdiction: From a Contacts to an Interest Analysis","tp":["court"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss1/1","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["David M. Lawrence"],"ab":"The purpose of this Article is to investigate the constitutional boundaries that surround the most common forms of governmental participation. Part II of the Article discusses the constitutional limitations on property transactions in which the government either uses its power of eminent domain to condemn land for private downtown development, acquires land for the same purpose through a voluntary sale by the owner of the land, or subsidizes private development by its method of conveying property to the developer. Part III of the Article then discusses the problems that arise when a downtown project includes both public and private facilities, and the public facilities arguably benefit the private facilities far more than they do the general public. Finally, the Article concludes by briefly summarizing the actual or probable resolution of the major legal issues in each of the topic areas.","c":0,"i":2,"id":"vlr-5615","j":"vlr","kw":["constitutional boundaries","property transactions","eminent domain","Constitutional Law","Law","Property Law and Real Estate"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2861/viewcontent/18_35VandLRev277_1982_.pdf","t":"Constitutional Limitations on Governmental Participation in Downtown Development Projects","tp":["takings"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss2/1","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"wlulr-9197","j":"wlulr","kw":["Search and seizure","Constitutional Law","Law Enforcement and Corrections"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2606/viewcontent/73_39Wash_26LeeLRev1437_281982_29.pdf","t":"Stop And Frisk Based Upon Anonymous Telephone Tips","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss4/10","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"wlulr-9198","j":"wlulr","kw":["Abortion/Public funds","Health Law and Policy"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2608/viewcontent/75_39Wash_26LeeLRev1469_281982_29.pdf","t":"Abortion Funding Restrictions: State Constitutional Protections Exceed Federal Safeguards","tp":["dueprocess","statecon"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss4/12","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"wlulr-9201","j":"wlulr","kw":["Incompetents","Sterilization (Birth control)","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Disability Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2569/viewcontent/18_39Wash_26LeeLRev207_281982_29.pdf","t":"The Sterilization Rights Of Mental Retardates","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss1/12","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"wlulr-9202","j":"wlulr","kw":["Collective bargaining","Labor and Employment Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2573/viewcontent/22_39Wash_26LeeLRev285_281982_29.pdf","t":"First National Maintenance Corp. V. Nlrb: The Supreme Court Narrows Employers' Section 8(A)(5) Duty To Bargain","tp":["court"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss1/16","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"wlulr-9199","j":"wlulr","kw":["Estoppel","Civil rights","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Criminal Procedure"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2609/viewcontent/76_39Wash_26LeeLRev1491_281982_29.pdf","t":"The Collateral Estoppel Effect Of Guilty Pleas In Section 1983 Actions","tp":["remedies","equality"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss4/13","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":["David A. Yuckman"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"wlulr-9200","j":"wlulr","kw":["Employment discrimination/Handicapped persons","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Disability Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2563/viewcontent/12_39Wash_26LeeLRev69_281982_29.pdf","t":"Employment Discrimination And The Visually Impaired","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss1/6","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":["Mark P. Gergen"],"ab":"","c":0,"i":1,"id":"uclrev-2996","j":"uclrev","pdf":"https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/context/uclrev/article/4264/viewcontent/49UChiLRev156.pdf","t":"Constitutional Limitations on State Long Arm Jurisdiction","tp":["general"],"u":"https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/uclrev/vol49/iss1/5","v":49,"y":1982},{"a":["Frank I. Goodman"],"ab":"Paul Brest, as always, has powerfully illuminated his subject. His analysis of the state action problem is searching, subtle, arid generally sound. The part I find most intriguing, however, is thie one with which I least agree. I refer to Brest's attempt to establish conceptual and historical connections between the state action doctrine and two substantive constitutional theories, positivism and natural law, representing polar tendencies in liberal thought. My comments will be addressed primarily to this aspect of Brest's paper. I also comment briefly on Professor Stone's excellent discussion of governmental tort immunity.","c":18,"fp":1331,"i":6,"id":"pennlr-21611","j":"pennlr","t":"Professor Brest on State Action and Liberal Theory, and a Postscript to Professor Stone","tp":["remedies","jurisprudence"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311973","v":130,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/professor-brest-on-state-action-and-liberal-theory-and-a-postscript-to-professor-stone/149469","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["David B. Filvaroff"],"ab":"Unlikely Heroes is designed for both the lawyer and the general reader. It does not purport to offer careful legal analysis of constitutional doctrine or to measure the Fifth Circuit's actions during this era against any articulated concept of the proper role of the judiciary in a federal system. To the extent that Bass raises any questions about the propriety of the judges' conduct or decisions, he appears to conclude that they were fully justified by the nature of the issues presented and by the demands of the times.Whatever its intended audience, however, the book would have benefited from a less erratic use of citations and the inclusion of additional detail on a number of the cases.","i":5,"id":"vlr-5622","j":"vlr","kw":["race relations","civil rights revolution","judges of the Fifth Circuit","Brown v. Board of Education","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Judges","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2889/viewcontent/55_35VandLRev1251_1982_.pdf","t":"Book Review: Unlikely Heroes by Jack Bass","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss5/5","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":[],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"wlulr-9196","j":"wlulr","kw":["Age discrimination","Civil Rights and Discrimination"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2603/viewcontent/70_39Wash_26LeeLRev1371_281982_29.pdf","t":"Striking A Balance Between The Interests Of Public Safety And The Rights Of Older Workers: The Age Bfoq Defense","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss4/7","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":["C.M.A. McCauliff"],"ab":"This Article analyzes the whole range of burdens of proof as well as their constitutional implications. Part H of the Article discusses the traditional burdens of proof and the use of probability theory in legal fact finding. Part HI of the Article studies the decision making processes of law enforcement officers, the judges that review their decisions, and the decision making processes in appellate and administrative review. Part IV of the Article returns to the trial process and analyzes burdens of proof, not as degrees of belief, but as reflections of constitutional due process that mandate a required degree of belief for the trial of certain important rights.Part V of the Article reports the results of a survey of judges on the meaning of various burdens of proof. Finally, the Article concludes that the objective-subjective dichotomy is an attempt to ex-press at pretrial and post trial stages the same policy concern that the Supreme Court has set forth at the trial level: the importance of the interests at stake. The proliferation of new burdens of proof,however, obscures and…","i":6,"id":"vlr-5623","j":"vlr","kw":["burden of proof","evidence","search and seizure","Evidence","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2891/viewcontent/59_35VandLRev1293_1982_.pdf","t":"Burdens of Proof: Degrees of Belief, Quanta of Evidence, or Constitutional Guarantees?","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss6/1","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["Elizabeth J. Schwartz"],"ab":"This Recent Development examines the current conflict among the circuits. This study first explores the rationales under-lying use immunity and contrasts them with the guidelines formulated by the Supreme Court for controlling judicial disclosure of grand jury testimony. Second, this Recent Development examines the analyses used by the federal courts in determining whether to release immunized grand jury testimony for civil use's and the effect of disclosure on a witness' claim of fifth amendment privilege.This study submits that in their effort to promote civil discovery,several courts have misconstrued the scope and effect of the disclosure power and have usurped the prosecutor's exclusive right under the federal statute to grant immunity. This Recent Development suggests alternative guidelines that courts might use in determining whether to release immunized grand jury testimony and the effect that disclosure should have on a court's evaluation of a witness' fifth amendment claim. The study concludes that courts should not allow disclosure and private litigants' use of immunized…","c":0,"i":5,"id":"vlr-5621","j":"vlr","kw":["immunized testimony","judicial disclosure","grand jury testimony","fifth amendment privilege","Criminal Law","Criminal Procedure","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2888/viewcontent/54_35VandLRev1211_1982_.pdf","t":"Disclosure and Civil Use of Immunized Testimony","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss5/4","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["Martin R. Gardner"],"ab":"This Article attempts to provide an analytical framework for identifying the punitive aspects of the juvenile justice system. The Article proposes a framework that is extrapolated from Supreme Court cases which define punishment in contexts outside the juvenile area. Several commentators have criticized the Court's definitional efforts, some because of perceived inadequacies in the developed definitions, others because of the belief that the very enterprise of defining constitutional rights in terms of the presence or absence of punishment is misguided . Although many of these criticisms of the Court's record are understandable, the alleged defects are less detrimental to an effective analysis of certain juvenile rights cases than they might be in other areas. Indeed, this Article argues that the Court's definitional framework is especially useful in the juvenile justice context.","i":4,"id":"vlr-5618","j":"vlr","kw":["punishment","youthful offenders","juvenile justice","eighth amendment","Constitutional Law","Juvenile Law","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2880/viewcontent/42_35VandLRev791_1982_.pdf","t":"Punishment and Juvenile Justice: A Conceptual Framework for Assessing Constitutional Rights of Youthful Offenders","tp":["court","crimpro"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss4/1","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["H. Richard Uviller"],"ab":"This Article isolates only two of the many aspects of the Court's labors affecting the acquisition of evidence for criminal prosecution. The first concerns the allocation of primacy among the values that the exclusionary response to the illegal acquisition of evidence serves: a theoretical choice that may carry some notable practical consequences. The second requires are examination of the role of the trial court in supervising the preaccusatory search for evidence in a way that suggests the possible obsolescence of the Supreme Court's ruling credo in the Stewart era.","i":3,"id":"vlr-5617","j":"vlr","kw":["evidence","criminal prosecution","rule of exclusion","fifth amendment","Criminal Law","Evidence","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2869/viewcontent/29_35VandLRev501_1982_.pdf","t":"The Acquisition of Evidence for Criminal Prosecution: Some Constitutional Premises and Practices in Transition","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss3/2","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["William R. Anderson"],"ab":"presents a central question about how usefully and how legitimately courts have dealt with the issues of state taxing powers. The United States Supreme Court has assumed a role as the principal architect of this component of federalism. State legislatures and tax officials have, of course, played roles, but they have always operated under the shadow of judicial doctrine. While Congress has not been wholly inactive, its role has been derivative, interstitial, and hesitant. Perhaps Congress' fact-finding role has been larger than its legislative role.'","c":1,"i":4,"id":"vlr-5620","j":"vlr","kw":["state taxing powers","federalism","due process","free trade doctrine","Law","Taxation-State and Local","Tax Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2884/viewcontent/47_35VandLRev1077_1982_.pdf","t":"Federal Limitations on State and Local Taxation","tp":["congress","federalism","dueprocess","econreg"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss4/5","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["David J. White"],"ab":"This Recent Development first considers the evolution of the Parker doctrine in a variety of contexts-with special attention to the Supreme Court's decision in Lafayette and the Court's rationale in Boulder. After discussing the recent lower federal court decisions that based the availability of the Parker exemption upon the existence of general home rule authority under the respective state's constitution, this Recent Development examines the problems posed by the Boulder decision for cities and states contemplating economic regulation tailored to particular local concerns. This Recent Development then analyzes the competing policy interests in the question of a home rule municipality's liability under the antitrust laws. In light of the Court's decision in Boulder to reject home rule authority as sufficient state authorization for an application of the Parker doctrine, this Recent Development concludes that the Supreme Court's approach in Boulder will be least burdensome for home rule municipalities if the Court utilizes a preemption rationale under the supremacy clause to avoid…","c":0,"i":4,"id":"vlr-5619","j":"vlr","kw":["antitrust","home rule authority","state action immunity","Antitrust and Trade Regulation","Law"],"pdf":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/context/vlr/article/2883/viewcontent/46_35VandLRev1041_1982_.pdf","t":"Municipalities and the Antitrust Laws: Home Rule Authority is Insufficient to Ensure State Action Immunity","tp":["remedies","court","econreg"],"u":"https://scholarship.law.vanderbilt.edu/vlr/vol35/iss4/4","v":35,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"This Note rejects these arguments in favor of the thesis that the census clause affirmatively requires including illegal aliens in the census figures used to apportion representatives among the states. Part I argues that the framers intended to allocate representation among the states based on a number of considerations, including wealth, and chose total population within the territory of each state as the best measure of those considerations. Part II contends that the requirement of individual equality in voting rights does not apply to interstate comparisons of voting power. Rather, a specific structural agreement reached by the states as sovereign entities governs the allocation of representation among them. Part III argues that while the question of counting illegal aliens in the census presents numerous technical and political issues subject to congressional discretion, the essential constitutional issues concern the interpretation of article I, section 2, especially the definition of \"person.\" These issues ultimately lie within the purview of the Supreme Court.","c":1,"i":6,"id":"mlr_mich-947","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Noncitizens","Territoriality","Apportionment","Census","Population","Constitutional Law","Immigration Law","Law and Politics"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4238/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"A Territorial Approach to Representation for Illegal Aliens","tp":["citizenship","districting","voting"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss6/8","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"Part I briefly identifies the considerations underlying the Stone Court's decision to limit habeas corpus review of fourth amendment claims. Part II then argues against applying Stone to the sixth amendment claim. After establishing the analytic difference between the two constitutional claims and examining Stone's \"opportunity for full and fair litigation\" standard, it concludes that Stone is fully consistent with free review of habeas corpus petitions alleging incompetent handling of fourth amendment questions. Finally, responding to a popular interpretation of Stone, Part II demonstrates that the possibility that ineffectiveness claims may not further the determination of a defendant's factual guilt or innocence should not preclude their review in habeas corpus proceedings.","i":6,"id":"mlr_mich-946","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Stone v. Powell","Effective assistance of counsel","Sixth Amendment","Habeas corpus","Incompetence","United States Supreme Court","Constitutional Law","Criminal Procedure"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4237/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Stone v. Powell and the Effective Assistance of Counsel","tp":["crimpro","security","court"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss6/7","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["William F. Duker"],"ab":"","c":15,"fp":1186,"i":5,"id":"hlr-14783","j":"hlr","t":"A Constitutional History of Habeas Corpus","tp":["security"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1340579","v":95,"y":1982},{"a":["Rogers M. Smith","Samuel P. Huntington"],"ab":"","c":26,"fp":1691,"i":7,"id":"hlr-14566","j":"hlr","t":"The \"American Creed\" and Constitutional Theory","tp":["method"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1340724","v":95,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"A Review of Trial and Error: The Detroit School Segregation Case by Eleanor P. Wolf","i":4,"id":"mlr_mich-945","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Public schools","Segregation","Race and law","Racism","Racial discrimination","Brown v. Board of Education","United States Supreme Court","Detroit"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4220/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Trial and Error: The Detroit School Segregation Case","tp":["equality","remedies","court"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss4/50","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["Morris S. Arnold"],"ab":"A Review of Treason Against God: A History of the Offense of Blasphemy by Leonard W. Levy","c":0,"i":4,"id":"mlr_mich-942","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["History","Blasphemy","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Legal History","Religion Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4199/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Legal History and the Law of Blasphemy","tp":["history","remedies","equality"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss4/29","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["Andrew S. Watson"],"ab":"A Review of The Enigma of Felix Frankfurter by H.N. Hirsch","c":0,"i":4,"id":"mlr_mich-941","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Frankfurter (Felix)","United States Supreme Court","Judges","Legal Biography","Supreme Court of the United States"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4196/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"A Psychohistorical View of Mr. Justice Frankfurter","tp":["court"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss4/26","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["David H. Kaye"],"ab":"A Review of Statistical Proof of Discrimination by David Baldus and James Cole","c":9,"i":4,"id":"mlr_mich-944","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Statistics","Proof","Discrimination","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Evidence","Litigation"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4208/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"The Numbers Game: Statistical Inference in Discrimination Cases","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss4/38","v":80,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"A Review of The Court Years, 1939-1975: The Autobiography of William O. Douglas by William O. Douglas","i":4,"id":"mlr_mich-943","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Douglas (William O.)","United States Supreme Court","Judges","Legal Biography","Supreme Court of the United States"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4202/viewcontent/uc.pdf","rv":1,"t":"The Court Years, 1939-1975: The Autobiography of William O. Douglas","tp":["court"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol80/iss4/32","v":80,"y":1982,"cx":1},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"This Note argues that the private cause of action under NEPA retains its utility despite the completion of the project sued upon. Part I describes the procedural implementation of the policy concerns underlying NEPA through the EIS process for proposed actions, and the EIS supplementation process for project changes made after the original EIS has been prepared. Part II examines current law applicable to projects completed in violation of NEPA and concludes that the denial of post-completion relief conflicts with the underlying goals of NEPA. Part III analyzes extension of relief to completed projects, and proposes court-ordered EIS supplementation for projects completed in violation of NEPA.","i":1,"id":"mlr_mich-1029","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["National Environmental Policy Act","Environmental impact statements","Judicial review","Federal agencies","Administrative Law","Environmental Law","Legislation"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4643/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"EIS Supplements for Improperly Completed Projects: A Logical Extension of Judicial Review Under NEPA","tp":["court","admin"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol81/iss1/6","v":81,"y":1982},{"a":["Kenneth Scott Schlesinger"],"ab":"","fp":1291,"i":5,"id":"calr-21377","j":"calr","t":"Polk County v. Dodson: Liability under Section 1983 for a Public Defender's Failure to Provide Adequate Counsel","tp":["remedies"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3480137","v":70,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://doi.org/10.15779/z38b16b"},{"a":["Bruce W. Blakely"],"ab":"Recent United States Supreme Court cases, while recognizing the potential dangers of corporate advocacy,' have upheld corporations' rights to engage in political expression through both political contributions and direct advocacy, and have struck down laws aimed at restricting such speech activity. 2In 1980, the Court expanded this corporate speech right by holding that a regulatory agency could not prevent a state-regulated public utility from using its billing envelopes to advocate nuclear power even though the company's monopoly status was conferred by the state and the corporation had a duty to operate in the public interest. 3 Given that state-created monopolies can now employ this unique and pervasive forum for political speech, the Court could have determined that these companies must be required to afford groups with opposing views a right to reply to the utilities' political statements.The Court, however, explicitly chose not to decide this issue. 4 This Comment addresses this unanswered question and argues that the first amendment requires utilities to afford opposing…","fp":1221,"i":5,"id":"calr-21356","j":"calr","t":"Public Utility Bill Inserts, Political Speech, and the First Amendment: A Constitutionally Mandated Right to Reply","tp":["speech","court","econreg"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3480135","v":70,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://doi.org/10.15779/z38kj0z"},{"a":["H. David Natkin"],"ab":"","c":0,"i":2,"id":"wlulr-9203","j":"wlulr","kw":["Civil Rights and Discrimination"],"pdf":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/context/wlulr/article/2591/viewcontent/40_39Wash_26LeeLRev628_281982_29.pdf","t":"VIII. Employment Discrimination & Affirmative Action","tp":["equality","remedies"],"u":"https://scholarlycommons.law.wlu.edu/wlulr/vol39/iss2/18","v":39,"y":1982},{"a":["A. James Melnick"],"ab":"Cable television has been heralded as a great opportunity for the public to receive programming both directly responsive to community needs and truly diverse in content.'This optimism stems from the technological capability of cable to deliver many more channels into the home than has been available over the unaided airwaves. 2 To take advantage of this capability, some legal commentators have favored government regulation requiring cable operators to provide facilities for community oriented programming through \"access channels\" that would be available to the public at little or no cost. 3oponents of cable access channels have sought to justify requiring such channels by employing the \"affirmative duty theory\" 4 of the first amendment.The affirmative duty theory posits that the first amendment requires the government to encourage the presentation of diverse ideas in the media.The Supreme Court arguably supported such a theory in a decision that allowed content regulation of the broadcasting industry by the Federal Communications Commission (FCC).'This decision recognized the…","fp":1393,"i":6,"id":"calr-21393","j":"calr","t":"Access to Cable Television: A Critique of the Affirmative Duty Theory of the First Amendment","tp":["speech","court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3480272","v":70,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://doi.org/10.15779/z387755"},{"a":["Kathryn A. Dunwoody"],"ab":"The propriety of the use of the initiative in connection with zoning decisions has been disputed in California for over fifty years.'In rnel Development Co. v. City of Costa Mesa, 2 the California Supreme Court decided that the initiative could be used to zone or rezone any property, regardless of its size.The court based its holding on the theory that any zoning or rezoning is a legislative act, and therefore a proper subject for initiative. 3 The question in Arnel arose at the confluence of two important rights.First, the California Constitution reserves the power of initiative in the people as legislators, and thus enables the direct passage of any measure so long as that measure is a proper subject for legislative determination. 4 Second, both the California 5 and federal 6 Constitutions guarantee that no one will be deprived of property without due process of law.The conjunction of these two doctrines presents hard policy questions.Answering them requires an examination of the policies behind allowing legislation-by-initiative and the desire for procedural fairness in land use…","fp":1107,"i":4,"id":"calr-21310","j":"calr","t":"Arnel Development Co. v. City of Costa Mesa: Rezoning by Initiative and Landowners' Due Process Rights","tp":["dueprocess","takings"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3480234","v":70,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://doi.org/10.15779/z38nb26"},{"a":["Walter Hellerstein"],"ab":"The first part of this Article, State Income Taxation of Multijurisdictional Corporations: Reflections on Mobil, Exxon, and H.R. 5076, did not contemplate a sequel. The Supreme Court's decisions last term in two state corporate income tax cases, however, created an irresistible opportunity to write one. The Court's opinions in ASARCO and Woolworth picked up where its opinions in Mobil and Exxon left off. Yet the direction taken by these more recent decisions veers sharply from the course ostensibly set by their predecessors. This Article will consider the Court's latest pronouncements in this area in a continuing if quixotic effort to discover where they may be leading. After analyzing the Court's decisions in ASARCO and Woolworth against the background of the uniform state law from which they arose, the Article explores the framework of state income taxation of multijurisdictional corporations in the wake of these decisions.","c":1,"i":1,"id":"mlr_mich-1031","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["ASARCO Inc. v. Idaho State Tax Comm.","F. W. Woolworth Co. v. Taxation and Revenue Dept.","State tax","Income tax","Corporate tax","Corporations","United States Supreme Court","Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4641/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"State Income Taxation of Multijurisdictional Corporations, Part II: Reflections on ASARCO and Woolworth","tp":["court","econreg"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol81/iss1/4","v":81,"y":1982},{"a":["Bruce J. Winick"],"ab":"As presently construed, the Constitution does not prohibit the death penalty. The states and the federal government may punish the commission of certain crimes with death, so long as the extreme penalty is not imposed on a mandatory basis and so long as the procedures used in imposing a death sentence meet constitutional scrutiny. A demonstration that the prosecutor used the peremptory challenge in the manner described in a single case probably would be insufficient to support a constitutional challenge in the federal courts and in the vast majority of state courts. In these courts a prosecutor's use of the peremptory in a single case is generally beyond review. To raise a constitutional challenge, a defendant may have to show a systematic pattern in the use of peremptories by prosecutors over a significant number of cases, including the removal of at least one juror opposed to the death penalty in the defendant's own case. To determine whether this practice by prosecutors does in fact exist, the author conducted a study of capital jury selection in Florida's Fourth Judicial…","i":1,"id":"mlr_mich-1030","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Empirical studies","Peremptory challenges","Voir dire","Prosecutors","Juries","Sixth Amendment","Death penalty","Capital punishment"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4639/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Prosecutorial Peremptory Challenge Practices in Capital Cases: An Empirical Study and a Constitutional Analysis","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol81/iss1/2","v":81,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"This Note suggests that a court faced with inadequate conciliation efforts by the EEOC should dismiss the action without prejudice. Part I argues that dismissal better serves the remedial purpose of the statute than summary judgment. Part II then demonstrates that dismissal satisfies the policy concerns of courts that dispose of inadequately conciliated suits. Although dismissal may not promote judicial efficiency as well as summary judgment, courts and the Commission can handle the dismissal to minimize duplication. Part III advances dismissal for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted as the appropriate procedural vehicle for disposing of actions when the Commission has failed to satisfy the conciliation requirement.","i":2,"id":"mlr_mich-1033","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Equal Employment Opportunity Commission","Equal Employment Opportunity Act","Employment discrimination","Conciliation","Administrative Law","Civil Rights and Discrimination","Labor and Employment Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4650/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Judicial Responses to the EEOC's Failure to Attempt Conciliation","tp":["equality","remedies","admin"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol81/iss2/4","v":81,"y":1982},{"a":["Michigan Law Review"],"ab":"This Note reformulates the doctrine of prosecutorial vindictiveness in light of the distinction drawn in Goodwin between pretrial and posttrial charging decisions. Part I recounts the development of the vindictiveness concept, and argues that in extending the doctrine beyond the factual settings which moved the Supreme Court to fashion its original prophylactic rule, the circuit courts have seriously eroded an essential due process safeguard. Part II critically examines the distinction between pretrial and posttrial charging decisions relied upon in Goodwin. Developing the logical corollary of the Goodwin holding, this Part argues that just as the pretrial situation does not warrant \"an inflexible presumption of vindictiveness\" when prosecutors substitute more serious charges, such a presumption is fully warranted after an initial trial. During this posttrial period, defendants' interests reach their apex and the government's interest in more serious charges significantly declines. Part III sets forth a revitalized prophylactic rule which reflects this balance, and responds to the…","i":1,"id":"mlr_mich-1032","j":"mlr_mich","kw":["Due process","United States v. Goodwin","Prosecutors","Sixth Amendment","Prosecutorial discretion","Fairness","United States Supreme Court","Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/context/mlr/article/4642/viewcontent/uc.pdf","t":"Prosecutorial Vindictiveness in the Criminal Appellate Process: Due Process Protection After United States v. Goodwin","tp":["dueprocess","crimpro","court"],"u":"https://repository.law.umich.edu/mlr/vol81/iss1/5","v":81,"y":1982},{"a":["Thomas K. Buck"],"ab":"","i":2,"id":"hclq-11733","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1238/viewcontent/17_9HastingsConstLQ351_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Self-Incrimination in Civil Litigation: The Evolution of California's Judicially Created Immunities from Murphy v. Waterfront Commission","tp":["crimpro"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss2/3","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["James W. Torke"],"ab":"","i":2,"id":"hclq-11732","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1237/viewcontent/16_9HastingsConstLQ279_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"The Judicial Process in Equal Protection Cases","tp":["equality"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss2/2","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Rosemary C. Salomone"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11735","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1256/viewcontent/07_10HastingsConstLQ15_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"Title VI and the Intent/Impact Debate: A Critical Look at Coextensiveness","tp":["general"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/3","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["Taunya Lovell Banks"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"hclq-11734","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1245/viewcontent/26_9HastingsConstLQ579_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"The Scope of Section 1985 (3) in Light of Great American Federal Savings and Loan Association v. Novotny: Too Little Too Late","tp":["general"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss3/5","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Louis H. Pollak","George L. Haskins","Herbert Alan Johnson"],"ab":"Chapter of the Order of the Coif conferred on George Lee Haskins its award for distinguished legal scholarship.The","fp":475,"i":2,"id":"pennlr-22161","j":"pennlr","t":"Marshall and the \"Campaign of History\"","tp":["general"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311855","v":131,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/marshall-and-the-campaign-of-history/149541","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Betty Hansen Richardson"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"hclq-11726","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1247/viewcontent/29_9HastingsConstLQ669_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Not a Closed Case: The Wisconsin Open Presidential Primary","tp":["executive"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss3/7","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Colette I. Hughes"],"ab":"","i":2,"id":"hclq-11725","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1240/viewcontent/19_9HastingsConstLQ429_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Liberty from Transfer Trauma: A Fundamental Life and Liberty Interest","tp":["dueprocess"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss2/5","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Wilbur Edel"],"ab":"","c":4,"fp":2010,"i":8,"id":"hlr-15285","j":"hlr","t":"A Constitutional Convention: Threat or Challenge?","tp":["history"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1340660","v":95,"y":1982},{"a":["Martin H. Redish"],"ab":"","c":68,"fp":591,"i":3,"id":"pennlr-21491","j":"pennlr","t":"The Value of Free Speech","tp":["speech"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311836","v":130,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/value-of-free-speech/149370","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Stanley C. Brubaker"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11738","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1257/viewcontent/08_10HastingsConstLQ81_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"From Incompetent Imperialism to Principled Prudence: The Role of the Courts in Restoring the State","tp":["general"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/4","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["John E. Bolmer, II"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11755","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1259/viewcontent/10_10HastingsConstLQ187_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"The Public Forum Doctrine and Haig v. Agee","tp":["speech"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/6","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["Richard Neely","Guido Calabresi"],"ab":"Common Law for the Age of Statutes I was reminded of Merlin's book of magic in Tennyson's Idylls","c":2,"fp":271,"i":1,"id":"pennlr-21783","j":"pennlr","t":"Obsolete Statutes, Structural Due Process, and the Power of Courts to Demand a Second Legislative Look","tp":["dueprocess"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311834","v":131,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/obsolete-statutes-structural-due-process-and-the-power-of-courts-to-demand-a-second-legislative-look/149522","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Bruce Nevin Shortt"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11754","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1258/viewcontent/09_10HastingsConstLQ145_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"The Establishment Clause and Religion-Based Categories: Taking Entanglement Seriously","tp":["takings","religion"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/5","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["Arthur J. Goldberg"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11753","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1255/viewcontent/06_10HastingsConstLQ7_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"The Supreme Court Reaches Out and Touches Someone--Fatally","tp":["court"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/2","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["Stanley Mosk"],"ab":"","i":1,"id":"hclq-11752","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1254/viewcontent/05_10HastingsConstLQ1_281982_1983_29.pdf","t":"A Tribute to Justice Arthur J. Goldberg","tp":["court"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol10/iss1/1","v":10,"y":1982},{"a":["William E. Nelson","George L. Haskins","Herbert Alan Johnson"],"ab":"","fp":489,"i":2,"id":"pennlr-21953","j":"pennlr","t":"Emulating the Marshall Court: The Applicability of the Rule of Law to Contemporary Constitutional Adjudication","tp":["democratic"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311856","v":131,"y":1982,"c":0},{"a":["Editors,"],"ab":"tion by applying the Sykes bar without even mentioning the \"cause and prejudice\" exception. 54 Relying on Chief Justice Burger's concurring opinion in Sykes, most courts discussing the \"cause and prejudice\" standard 55","fp":981,"i":4,"id":"pennlr-22025","j":"pennlr","t":"Federal Habeas Corpus Review of Unintentionally Defaulted Constitutional Claims","tp":["security"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311934","v":130,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/federal-habeas-corpus-review-of-unintentionally-defaulted-constitutional-claims/149420","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Editors,"],"ab":"Supreme Court opinions at the turn of the century established one of the fundamental rules of federal Indian law: Congress has a broad \"plenary power\" to administer the property and affairs of Indian tribes.In Lone Wolf v. Hitchcock,\" the Court characterized the plenary power as \"a political one, not subject to be controlled by the judicial department of the government.\"' As a matter of constitutional law, the Lone Wolf plenary authority rests on vague and extraordinarily unexamined foundations.The courts have accepted without question the proposition that Congress has the constitutional authority to manage internal tribal affairs as well as to define the relationship between states, tribes, and the federal government. 3Indian tribes traditionally have been unsuccessful in litigation involving the plenary power rule because their aboriginal and treaty rights generally are not recognized as limits upon this federal power. 4 During the past century, the courts' deference to federal Indian legislation has permitted frequent and radical shifts in federal policy often with disastrous…","c":1,"fp":235,"i":1,"id":"pennlr-21812","j":"pennlr","t":"Federal Plenary Power in Indian Affairs after Weeks and Sioux Nation","tp":["comparative","citizenship","congress"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311833","v":131,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/federal-plenary-power-in-indian-affairs-after-weeks-and-sioux-nation/149507","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Jonathan C. Medow"],"ab":"","c":1,"fp":775,"i":4,"id":"pennlr-21847","j":"pennlr","t":"The First Amendment and the Secrecy State: Snepp V. United States","tp":["speech"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311929","v":130,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/first-amendment-and-the-secrecy-state-snepp-v.-united-states/149398","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Martin H. Redish"],"ab":"There is something of an irony in Professor Baker's decision to respond to my theory of the value of free speech. Of the modern free speech theorists with whom I differ-and the number is considerable '-Professor Baker is in many ways the one with whom I have the fewest fundamental differences. Indeed, much of my article was structured as a defense to an anticipated attack by those theorists who believe that the sole, or at least primary, function of the free speech guarantee is to further the political process.2 Professor Baker is undoubtedly the one least concerned with this issue. Instead, he emphasizes, as I do, the need to focus on development of the individual as the ultimate value of the first amendment guarantee.3 Yet my differences with Professor Baker are not insignificant. For, as I assert in my article, I believe that Baker fails to recognize the logical implications of his own fundamental assumptions.4 Professor Baker's response to my article consists of both a critique of my theory and a defense of his theory against the criticisms I made of it. In an important sense,…","c":4,"fp":678,"i":3,"id":"pennlr-21653","j":"pennlr","t":"Self-Realization, Democracy, and Freedom of Expression: A Reply to Professor Baker","tp":["speech","democratic"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311838","v":130,"y":1982,"oa":"https://repository.law.upenn.edu/Documents/Detail/self-realization-democracy-and-freedom-of-expression-a-reply-to-professor-baker/149379","oav":"publishedVersion"},{"a":["Jordan J. Paust"],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"hclq-11712","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1249/viewcontent/32_9HastingsConstLQ719_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Is the President Bound by the Supreme Law of the Land--Foreign Affairs and National Security Reexamined","tp":["security","executive"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss4/1","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Ralph U. Whitten"],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"hclq-11714","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1251/viewcontent/34_9HastingsConstLQ851_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"The Constitutional Limitations on State Choice of Law: Due Process","tp":["dueprocess"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss4/3","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Arthur J. Goldberg"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"hclq-11713","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1241/viewcontent/22_9HastingsConstLQ459_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"A Tribute to Justice Abe Fortas","tp":["court"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss3/1","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Susan G. Kupfer"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"hclq-11711","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1242/viewcontent/23_9HastingsConstLQ463_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Restructuring the Monroe Doctrine: Current Litigation under Section 1983","tp":["remedies"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss3/2","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Richard A. Posner"],"ab":"","c":39,"i":2,"id":"uclrev-2997","j":"uclrev","pdf":"https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/context/uclrev/article/4275/viewcontent/49UChiLRev263.pdf","t":"Economics, Politics, and the Reading of Statutes and the Constitution","tp":["general"],"u":"https://chicagounbound.uchicago.edu/uclrev/vol49/iss2/2","v":49,"y":1982},{"a":["Charles W. Sherman"],"ab":"Over the last decade abortion has been one of the most controversial issues in American life.In Committee to Defend Reproductive Rights v. Myers (CDRR), the abortion funding issue reached the California Supreme Court.The court faced a challenge to provisions in California's 1980 Budget Act 2 that severely restricted the funding of abortions in the Medi-Cal program, the state medical services program for indigents. 3 Analyzing the selective funding of a constitutional right in a general benefit program as an unconstitutional condition, the court held that the abortion funding restrictions in the Medi-Cal program, which resulted in the funding of childbirth but not abortion, infringed the woman's exercise of her right of procreative choice.The court then applied a special standard of review for conditioned benefit programs, 4 and held that the asserted state interests were not sufficient to justify the infringement.Thus, the court struck down the abortion funding restrictions.This Note proceeds as follows.Part I summarizes the CDR facts 1. 29 Cal.3d 252, 625 P.2d 779, 172 Cal.Rptr.866…","fp":978,"i":4,"id":"calr-21406","j":"calr","t":"Committee to Defend Reproductive Rights v. Myers: Abortion Funding Restrictions as an Unconstitutional Condition","tp":["dueprocess","court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3480229","v":70,"y":1982,"c":0,"oa":"https://doi.org/10.15779/z38917d"},{"a":["Abram J. Chayes"],"ab":"","c":17,"fp":1,"i":1,"id":"hlr-14709","j":"hlr","t":"The Supreme Court, 1981 Term","tp":["court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1340770","v":96,"y":1982},{"a":["C. Herman Pritchett","Bruce Allen Murphy"],"ab":"","c":5,"fp":1281,"i":5,"id":"pennlr-21649","j":"pennlr","t":"The Brandeis/Frankfurter Connection: The Secret Political Activities of Two Supreme Court Justices","tp":["court"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/3311807","v":130,"y":1982},{"a":["Inga Markovits"],"ab":"","c":38,"fp":513,"i":3,"id":"slr-22200","j":"slr","t":"Law or Order: Constitutionalism and Legality in Eastern Europe","tp":["comparative","democratic"],"u":"https://doi.org/10.2307/1228484","v":34,"y":1982},{"a":["Karen Havilan"],"ab":"","i":4,"id":"hclq-11721","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/article/1253/viewcontent/36_9HastingsConstLQ975_281981_1982_29.pdf","t":"Jurisdiction Meets the Press: First Amendment Considerations in Jurisdictional Analysis","tp":["speech"],"u":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/hastings_constitutional_law_quaterly/vol9/iss4/5","v":9,"y":1982},{"a":["Leon Friedman"],"ab":"","i":3,"id":"hclq-11720","j":"hclq","kw":["Constitutional Law"],"pdf":"https://repository.uclawsf.edu/context/hastings_constitu